WEBVTT

00:00.500 --> 00:03.426
A data center number is only useful if you
can trace it.

00:03.890 --> 00:04.923
Where did it come from?

00:05.109 --> 00:06.026
What does it measure?

00:06.491 --> 00:07.886
And what is still unknown?

00:14.500 --> 00:18.796
WattDocket brings public records, annual
disclosures and grid context

00:18.831 --> 00:19.446
together.

00:19.910 --> 00:23.892
The explorer now holds four hundred
seventeen annual values.

00:24.624 --> 00:27.062
Here is New York's Western Union Building.

00:27.688 --> 00:32.402
Every figure keeps its year, unit, source
and reporting boundary.

00:33.134 --> 00:36.115
Building and campus totals keep their own
scope.

00:38.300 --> 00:43.588
Here is Vantage VA sixteen in Sterling,
Virginia, linked to its EPA

00:43.680 --> 00:44.390
identity.

00:45.007 --> 00:48.934
Its actual electricity demand and water
use remain unavailable in the

00:48.991 --> 00:50.067
collected evidence.

00:50.787 --> 00:53.066
WattDocket leaves those gaps visible.

00:53.786 --> 00:57.707
A listing establishes identity, not
measured electricity use.

00:58.300 --> 01:02.690
The permit section quotes three limits:
twenty-nine hours a year for

01:02.747 --> 01:08.072
maintenance testing, five hundred hours
for all purposes, and six hundred

01:08.153 --> 01:12.693
eighty-five thousand, three hundred
twenty-five gallons of diesel a year.

01:13.420 --> 01:17.232
Open a condition to see the quote, page
and original document.

01:17.856 --> 01:20.697
These conditions cover the cited equipment
or activity.

01:21.230 --> 01:25.696
They do not establish actual fuel use or
whole-site limits.

01:26.600 --> 01:30.710
For the Texas record, I'll ask Meta Muse
Spark to explain the published

01:30.768 --> 01:33.113
estimate through the Meta Model API.

01:38.102 --> 01:41.295
It identifies nineteen thousand
twenty-five tonnes of modeled annual

01:41.341 --> 01:45.428
emissions and distinguishes that estimate
from a facility measurement.

01:46.159 --> 01:49.305
WattDocket checks the answer's numbers
against published facts.

01:49.770 --> 01:52.534
The source and the assumptions still need
review.

02:00.400 --> 02:04.127
The historical grid calculator shows the
assumptions behind that estimate:

02:04.754 --> 02:08.260
ten megawatts at sixty-five percent
utilization.

02:08.887 --> 02:13.810
That's fifty-six thousand, nine hundred
forty megawatt-hours a year.

02:14.658 --> 02:16.980
The calculation and source are here to
inspect.

02:17.374 --> 02:21.415
These are hypothetical results using a
historical regional factor, not the

02:21.461 --> 02:23.057
facility's measured footprint.

02:24.300 --> 02:26.331
The coverage page makes the gaps visible.

02:26.794 --> 02:29.858
Two thousand, two hundred thirty-four
locations need review.

02:30.589 --> 02:33.547
Reviewed disclosure links have also
expanded to twenty-eight.

02:34.279 --> 02:38.258
Missing values remain unavailable, and
each source keeps its reporting

02:38.328 --> 02:39.693
period and collection date.

02:40.300 --> 02:44.875
Visit wattdocket dot com, and inspect the
public code at github dot com

02:44.966 --> 02:48.183
slash pankajarm slash wattdocket dash
core.

02:48.704 --> 02:51.702
Built with Meta Muse Spark through the
Meta Model API.
